AIRK Service @VERSION@ â€” Linux x86-64

This package installs AIRK Service for your account: the airk service and the
airkctl command-line client. It contains no VSIX and does not install or open
VS Code; the VS Code extension is a separate download that connects to it.

This Linux package is unsigned. SHA256SUMS, the published release record and
GitHub build provenance are integrity and provenance checks, not an OS code
signature. install.sh verifies every packaged file against digests pinned in
the script before it runs any packaged program.

For an artifact published by the attestation-enabled GitHub release workflow,
verify its build provenance before extracting it:

  gh attestation verify @ARCHIVE_NAME@ -R iokaio/airk \
    --signer-workflow iokaio/airk/.github/workflows/github-release.yml \
    --deny-self-hosted-runners

A locally generated package has no attestation. Installation itself is offline.
GitHub CLI reference: https://cli.github.com/manual/gh_attestation_verify

Install (no sudo):

  tar -xzf @ARCHIVE_NAME@
  cd @DIRECTORY_NAME@
  ./install.sh            # or ./install.sh --help for the options

install.sh asks for the program folder and the index data, cache and log
locations (defaults below; a later install keeps the locations in use), then
shows what AIRK setup found. Every earlier AIRK installation is stopped and
removed with its indexes, which must be rebuilt; no source file is read,
changed or deleted, and coding agents must be reconnected. Setup asks before
it proceeds; an unattended install (--silent; --yes remains an alias) needs --acknowledge-reset when an
earlier installation is found.

  Program folder  ~/.local/lib/airk-service   (bin/airkctl, service/airk, notices/)
  Index data      ~/.local/share/airk/indexes
  Service state   ~/.local/state/airk/service
  Cache           ~/.cache/airk/service
  Logs            ~/.local/state/airk/service-logs

Each folder must belong to your account, must not be writable by others and
must not be a link; setup refuses folders that hold other content.

Login startup is one systemd user unit (skip it with --no-login-startup).
Certificate trust for the HTTPS admin pages is not automatic on Linux. When
~/.local/bin exists, install.sh adds an airkctl link there and records that it
owns it; otherwise it prints the airkctl path. No shell startup file is edited.

After installation:

  airkctl service status
  airkctl admin open

Running install.sh again repairs or upgrades the installation in place and
keeps the indexes. To uninstall, run:

  airkctl service uninstall --yes                  # keeps index data
  airkctl service uninstall --yes --purge-indexes  # also deletes it

Uninstall removes the login unit, the install record and pointer, the packaged
files and the owned airkctl link. Removing the extracted download alone does
not uninstall AIRK Service.

Requirements: Linux x86-64, a POSIX shell and GNU coreutils/findutils.
No Python, jq, network download or editor is required by this installer.

Headless --silent never reads stdin or asks for certificate trust. Trust is
skipped; request it later through the native interactive consent flow. A setup
or PATH/link failure exits nonzero and prints a persistent private recovery
folder containing the original request and verified setup image. Preserve it
and check service status before attempting recovery. Resume with the exact same
installer payload and original request:

  ./install.sh --resume "<absolute retained operation-request.json>"

Add --silent only when the original operation did not request consent. Do not
supply new locations/options, edit the JSON or replace it with a new request.
This also resumes an original native uninstall request retained by airkctl;
completed native work is reconciled before remaining wrapper work runs.
The portable CLI --resume command is reserved for portable setup requests.
Unicode paths are accepted.
